 "Bopomofo in Brazil: the 37 symbols and tones for families teaching Mandarin": 1,
 "Many families of Taiwanese origin in São Paulo and other cities want their children to read the way children in Taiwan do. The first step there is neither pinyin nor characters: it is bopomofo.": 1,
 "Zhuyin Games: Chinese Phonics": 1,
 "Bopomofo, also called zhuyin, is the system of 37 phonetic symbols used in Taiwan to teach Mandarin pronunciation before characters. There are 21 initials, 3 medials and 13 finals, plus marks for the four tones and the neutral tone. If your family has ties to Taiwan or your child will use books and materials from there, start with bopomofo; if the focus is mainland China, pinyin is the standard. At home, ten minutes a day, listening before writing, is enough to begin.": 1,
 "The big advantage of bopomofo for a Brazilian child is that the symbols do not look like Latin letters. With pinyin, a child tends to read \"x\", \"q\" or \"zh\" the Portuguese way and the pronunciation comes out wrong. With bopomofo, each symbol is a new sound, with no interference from the alphabet they are learning at school.": 1,
 "Tones are the hardest part for children growing up with Portuguese, because in Portuguese intonation changes the emotion of a sentence, not the meaning of a word. In Mandarin, mā, má, mǎ and mà are different words. That is why it pays to train the ear for tones from the start, with gestures or pictures that rise, fall or curve, rather than leaving them for later.": 1,
 "Once the child recognises single symbols, the next step is combining an initial, a medial and a final into a syllable, for example ㄍ + ㄨ + ㄚ. That is where reading really begins. Reviewing every day the symbols that still cause confusion works better than racing through all 37.": 1,
