 "Brown noise is deeper than white or pink noise and suits masking traffic or condo neighbours. How to choose, set a sensible volume and use a fade-out timer.": 1,
 "Brown noise vs white and pink noise: which one should you pick?": 1,
 "If white noise has ever felt too hissy to fall asleep to, try brown noise. It is deeper and softer, like a distant waterfall or a low, unbroken rumble of thunder.": 1,
 "White & Brown Noise, Rain, Fan": 1,
 "Brown noise is noise where low frequencies are louder than high ones, so it sounds deeper and heavier than white noise (equal energy at every frequency, heard as a sharp hiss) and pink noise (in between, like steady rain). Brown noise is good for masking low sounds such as traffic, motorbikes or a neighbour's TV, and as background sound for work. Evidence that it deepens sleep is still limited, so try it yourself for a week, play it as quietly as still masks the disturbance, and set a timer that fades it out.": 1,
 "The three colours differ in how sound energy is spread. White noise has equal energy at every frequency, and because our ears are sensitive to highs, it sounds like a sharp hiss. Pink noise reduces the highs somewhat so it sounds more balanced. Brown noise reduces them further, leaving a thick, low sound that many people find they can listen to longer without ear fatigue.": 1,
 "Choose by the sound you want to cover. Traffic on a main road, an air-conditioner compressor or bass from next door are low sounds that brown noise masks better. For voices or intermittent high sounds, pink noise or mixing in rain may work better. Many people in Thailand who already sleep with a fan on can try adding quiet brown noise to the fan sound instead of turning anything up.": 1,
 "Volume matters more than colour. Keep it low enough that you can still hear your surroundings a little, no louder than a gentle shower, and place the speaker away from your pillow. For babies, common guidance is to stay below about 50 decibels and keep the device away from the cot. Letting the sound fade after you fall asleep, rather than playing it all night, also limits continuous exposure.": 1,
