 "When a child won't brush, dawdles over dinner or leaves toys out, most parents explain it again. For a three- to seven-year-old, more talking makes it the adult's job, not theirs.": 1,
 "Kids Reward & Chore Chart": 1,
 "Habits at three to seven come from keeping it small, short and immediate: practise one daily task at a time, split it into two or three visible steps, give specific encouragement the moment each step is done, and do it at the same time every day. Add the next task only once this one happens for a few weeks without reminders.": 1,
 "Children this age struggle to persist for something later; what matters is a response now. A reward that arrives at the weekend is often too far away for a three- or four-year-old. It works better to name the action right as it is finished: \"You put your toothbrush back in the cup yourself\" tells a child what to repeat far better than \"good job\". Rewards can be small, but they should be quick and tied to the behaviour.": 1,
 "A habit is a practised sequence, not an explanation given once. Start with the task most likely to succeed, such as tidying toys or brushing before bed, fix it to the same moment each day, and break it into pieces like \"toothpaste, brush for two minutes, rinse\". Do it together for the first week or two, then only prompt the first step. Starting three or four new habits at once tends to make both child and parent give up; stabilise one, then add the next.": 1,
