 "Fix a blurry photo: camera shake, missed focus or WhatsApp compression?": 1,
 "Not every blurry photo is the same. A shaky shot, an out-of-focus one and one that arrived soft via WhatsApp look alike on screen, but each has a different cause and a different fix.": 1,
 "First check where the photo came from. If it arrived via WhatsApp, ask for the original file (sent as a document or in HD), because compression removes detail no edit fully brings back. Then identify the blur: streaks in one direction mean camera shake, evenly soft edges mean missed focus, and coloured grain means low-light noise. Mild and moderate blur usually improves well with sharpening, noise removal or super-resolution; a face that is only a few pixels in a badly shaken photo will not become exact again, and the tool reconstructs detail rather than recovering the original.": 1,
 "WhatsApp reduces resolution and compresses photos to send them faster. For family, party and graduation photos that circulate through group chats, this is the most common cause of a \"blurry photo\". It is best to fix the largest version available: the original on the phone of whoever took it, or the file resent as a document. Fixing a copy of a copy multiplies the artefacts.": 1,
 "Shake and missed focus need different treatment. In a shaky photo, edges look doubled or dragged in one direction, and sharpening aimed at that kind of blur helps most. In an out-of-focus photo the background may be sharp while the face is soft; the gain is smaller there and depends on how much detail still exists. In night shots the problem is usually noise, and sharpening alone makes the grain worse: reduce noise first.": 1,
 "AI super-resolution tools generate plausible detail from what is in the image. That is great for making a photo presentable, but not as proof of who is in it or for reading an illegible number plate. Always compare before and after at full size, and be suspicious of overly smooth skin or text that \"appears\" where there was nothing before.": 1,
